<article class="env-lemma" id="07KE">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07KE">Lemma <span data-tag="07KE">60.2.5</span>.</a> Let $(A, I, \gamma )$ be a divided power ring. Let $B$ be an $A$-algebra and $IB \subset J \subset B$ an ideal. Let $x_ i$ be a set of variables. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  D_{B[x_ i], \gamma }(JB[x_ i] + (x_ i)) = D_{B, \gamma }(J) \langle x_ i \rangle  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      One possible proof is to deduce this from Lemma <a href="/tag/07HC" data-tag="07HC">60.2.4</a> as any relation between $x_ i$ in $B[x_ i]$ is trivial. On the other hand, the lemma follows from the universal property of the divided power polynomial algebra and the universal property of divided power envelopes. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>